{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/lint/timing.mjs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T18:59:10.284494Z","id":"b07b6a521baa4f4e45181f70a6a6179d24555f02aa9f1e9184ebd782a62eaa58","body":{"detail":"`numeric` (line 128) returns null for any identifier named in HANG_GUARDS (line 32) before it looks the name up in the file's own constants, and `hasHangGuard` accepts any member or binary expression that mentions one. So `const WAIT_MS = 50; await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, WAIT_MS))` (timer-delay), `{ timeout: HANG_MS / 100 }` (literal-timeout) and `while (Date.now() < start + WAIT_MS / 1000)` (clock-arithmetic) are all unreported: a sleep and two budgets under a guard's name. Files already give these names values of their own: web/e2e/chrome-overlay.spec.ts:31-32 (90 s and 300 s, where web/playwright.config.ts:29-30 has 110 s and 120 s), apps/clax_app/home/tests/home.test.js:26 (`HANG_MS = 9000`), and web/bridge/src/harness.ts:36 exports a production `WAIT_MS = 2000` that a test importing it would be exempt with. web/lint/timing.test.ts has no case for a guard the file declares itself or for a guard divided.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":77},"end":{"line":86},"content_hash":"0e6a5adaf3f18c2f3f0423ba5f46712dab1d8730adc2acaf48f7f952fbbab45a"},"suggested_fix":"Tie the exemption to where the value comes from: accept the names only when imported from the modules that define the suites' guards (web/shell/src/test/timeouts.ts, crates/clax-worker/harness.mjs, and one e2e module exporting Playwright's), or, for a file's own declaration, only when `declarations` finds its value at or above a floor (say 60_000), reporting the declaration otherwise. In `hasHangGuard` accept `guard`, `guard * n` and `guard + n` only, not `/` or `-`. Add both cases to timing.test.ts.","summary":"A hang guard is recognized by its name alone, so a file's own `const WAIT_MS = 50` passes every timing rule","tags":["review","review:deterministic-tests-2026-11-12","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/lint/timing.mjs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T18:59:10.284519Z","id":"cdea0a76b62b62102e205596cd0116e5900029c8a204d1cb83de2dd2b61bfb42","body":{"detail":"The rule reports `timeout: n`, `setTimeout(n)`, `setDefaultTimeout(n)` and a numeric last argument of it/test/describe or a hook. Budgets it does not see, each with an instance in the linted files: (1) `vi.setConfig({ testTimeout: 20_000 })` at web/bridge/test/pick.test.ts:5, web/bridge/test/target.test.ts:4 and web/bridge/test/comment-mode.test.ts:6, a third of the 60 s MOUNT_TIMEOUT_MS that web/vitest.config.ts gives every other test; (2) a bound passed by position: `ui.waitFor(fn, HANG_MS)` with `HANG_MS = 9000` at apps/clax_app/home/tests/home.test.js:26-27 (`vi.waitFor(fn, 3000)` would pass the same way); (3) limits written inside a string the page evaluates: `runTests({ ..., testMs: 10000, totalMs: 60000 })` at web/e2e/home-app.spec.ts:51, which is why home.test.js can wait 9 s at most. (2) and (3) leave every home app test on a 10 s wall-clock limit of its own: one whose condition holds later than that on a loaded machine fails. web/e2e/inspect-tests.spec.ts asks for the same harness's longest limits (its TOTAL_MAX_MS and MIGRATE_MAX_MS) for that reason, and web/e2e/.qual records the suite's other remaining real-time dependencies, but not this one.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":239},"end":{"line":258},"content_hash":"87c32868ee7c5a1c27446c7fef1b6289c913fee47f110bb268e573906a82ee8a"},"suggested_fix":"Report a numeric, non-guard value of `testTimeout`, `hookTimeout`, `actionTimeout` and `navigationTimeout`, and a numeric second argument of `waitFor`/`waitUntil`, with RuleTester cases. Remove the three `vi.setConfig` lines or name a guard there. In web/e2e/home-app.spec.ts:51 ask for the harness's maxima (testMs 30000, totalMs 120000: TEST_MAX_MS and TOTAL_MAX_MS, web/bridge/src/harness.ts:33-34), raise home.test.js's bound with it, and record the real-time limit that remains.","summary":"literal-timeout sees only the key `timeout` and a runner's last argument: `testTimeout`, a wait bound passed by position and limits inside evaluated strings pass","tags":["review","review:deterministic-tests-2026-11-12","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/lint/timing.mjs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T18:59:10.284541Z","id":"ed48f881837fde9684fa5bbcedd02192147d78d93ddc2202068c1d9925bd56b7","body":{"detail":"The exemption is a substring match on the source, so a comment, or one test's `vi.useFakeTimers()` undone by `vi.useRealTimers()`, turns the rule off for every timer in the file. Real-time timers that pass because of it today: web/shell/src/gallery.test.ts:28 (`setTimeout(r, 10)` in a polling helper; fake timers only in the test at lines 315-321), web/shell/src/view/spectator.test.ts:43 (`setTimeout(r, 5)`; fake timers only from lines 68 and 123), web/shell/src/caps/device.test.ts:251 (`setInterval(answer, 1)`; fake timers only in the tests at lines 379 and 419). These three are poll intervals, which elsewhere carry a directive with a reason (web/e2e/fixtures.ts:289); a `setTimeout(r, 500)` sleep added to any file that mentions fake timers would pass the same way. The header (lines 8-9) describes the exemption as for 'a file on vi.useFakeTimers, whose timers run on fake time', which these files are not at those lines.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":195},"end":{"line":196},"content_hash":"a45b94be221eecd498a4f29180652875948a8f7142f4fd167819c298662a2c10"},"suggested_fix":"Exempt a timer only where fake timers are in force: `vi.useFakeTimers()` called at module level or in a `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` of an enclosing describe, with no `vi.useRealTimers()` inside the file's tests, or earlier in the same test body. Report the rest and let each site carry its directive. Add RuleTester cases for a file that uses both kinds of time.","summary":"timer-delay is off for a whole file whenever its text contains `useFakeTimers(`, also for timers that run on real time","tags":["review","review:deterministic-tests-2026-11-12","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/lint/timing.mjs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T19:02:42.081005Z","id":"bb8eb03c02a48f376c9fea6cda69830cce1caba407fb02ff50088faa61df46cf","body":{"detail":"Traced in web/lint/timing.mjs. timer-delay: line 218 returns when `hasHangGuard(delay)`, and line 81 answers true for any identifier named in HANG_GUARDS (line 32), so `const WAIT_MS = 50; setTimeout(r, WAIT_MS)` is not reported; no rule reports the declaration. literal-timeout: `isBudget` (lines 143-146) asks `numeric`, whose line 128 returns null for a guard's name before `consts.get`, and lines 133-136 return null when either side is null, so `{ timeout: HANG_MS / 100 }` is no budget. clock-arithmetic: line 329 returns when `hasHangGuard(other)`, and line 83 accepts a binary expression of any operator with a guard on either side, so `Date.now() < start + WAIT_MS / 1000` is not reported. The names with values of their own: web/e2e/chrome-overlay.spec.ts:31-32 (90_000, 300_000), web/playwright.config.ts:29-30 (120_000, 110_000), apps/clax_app/home/tests/home.test.js:26 (`const HANG_MS = 9000`), web/bridge/src/harness.ts:36 (`export const WAIT_MS = 2000`). web/lint/timing.test.ts names a guard only in valid cases (lines 17-18, 34-35, 38, 56-57), none with a declaration in the file and none divided. The kind is right: a gap in a lint rule; nothing fails today.","kind":"comment","references":"b07b6a521baa4f4e45181f70a6a6179d24555f02aa9f1e9184ebd782a62eaa58","summary":"confirmed: all three rules exempt a guard by its name before reading the file's own value for it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/lint/timing.mjs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T19:02:42.081357Z","id":"2ca0f7a7d65cfcee2061772f8e9f6c4022b3872fce60cbe98fdeb524cbdd8f62","body":{"detail":"web/lint/timing.mjs:239-258 is the whole rule: line 241 reports a property only when its key is `timeout`; lines 248-250 take setTimeout(n), setDefaultTimeout and setDefaultNavigationTimeout; lines 254-257 take the last argument of a call rooted at RUNNERS or HOOKS. (1) `vi.setConfig({ testTimeout: 20_000 })` at web/bridge/test/pick.test.ts:5, target.test.ts:4 and comment-mode.test.ts:6: the key is `testTimeout` and the call is rooted at `vi`, so nothing matches; every other test gets MOUNT_TIMEOUT_MS = 60_000 (web/shell/src/test/timeouts.ts:11, web/vitest.config.ts:31). (2) apps/clax_app/home/tests/home.test.js:26-27: `const HANG_MS = 9000; const wait = (fn) => ui.waitFor(fn, HANG_MS)`: `waitFor` is no matched call, and the name is a guard's besides. (3) web/e2e/home-app.spec.ts:51: `runTests({ ..., testMs: 10000, totalMs: 60000 })` is inside a template string passed to `host(page, ...)`, which no AST rule reads; web/bridge/src/harness.ts:231-232 and 262-267 hold each test to testMs and the run to totalMs, and lines 33-34 allow up to 30_000 and 120_000. All three places are in the rule's scope (web/lint/timing.json: `**/bridge/test/**`, `**/clax_app/home/tests/**`, `**/*.spec.ts`). The branch itself wrote (2) (`git diff origin/main...HEAD -- apps/clax_app/home/tests/home.test.js`: 3000 became `HANG_MS = 9000`, 'just under the per-test limit the runner sets') and left the 10 s it sits under. `grep -rl testMs --include=.qual .` names web/lint/.qual only, where the one match is the record this replies to, so the limit is recorded nowhere else. The kind is right: a lint gap and a remaining real-time limit, with no failure shown.","kind":"comment","references":"cdea0a76b62b62102e205596cd0116e5900029c8a204d1cb83de2dd2b61bfb42","summary":"confirmed: literal-timeout matches the key `timeout`, one-argument setTimeout/setDefault*Timeout and a runner's or hook's last argument only, and each named budget is in a linted file","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/lint/timing.mjs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T19:02:42.081714Z","id":"59af0a2f4e4ca52f07ad81ea4639566f31b5907fe4be5709559ab0117c624f0d","body":{"detail":"web/lint/timing.mjs:196: `if (context.sourceCode.text.includes(\"useFakeTimers(\")) return {};` — a match on the source text, comments included, that returns no visitors for the file. Timers on real time in such files, none with a directive (`grep -n 'useFakeTimers\\|useRealTimers\\|setTimeout(\\|setInterval(\\|oxlint-disable'` on each): web/shell/src/gallery.test.ts:28 `setTimeout(r, 10)` in a module-level polling helper, fake timers only at lines 315-321 (useRealTimers at 321); web/shell/src/view/spectator.test.ts:43 `setTimeout(r, 5)`, fake timers from lines 68 and 123, useRealTimers at 59 and 127; web/shell/src/caps/device.test.ts:251 `setInterval(answer, 1)`, useRealTimers at 236, fake timers at 379 and 419. The same kind of real-time pause elsewhere carries its directive and reason (web/e2e/fixtures.ts:288-289). web/lint/timing.test.ts has one case for the exemption (line 19, `vi.useFakeTimers(); setTimeout(f, 500);`) and none for a file on both kinds of time. The header's wording is lines 8-9. The kind is right: a lint gap; the three timers are poll intervals, not results.","kind":"comment","references":"ed48f881837fde9684fa5bbcedd02192147d78d93ddc2202068c1d9925bd56b7","summary":"confirmed: one `useFakeTimers(` anywhere in a file's text turns timer-delay off for the file, and three files have real-time timers that pass by it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
